Z||000004||whamel||09-19-2000||02:40 PM||||    I wonder what kind of member numbers we would have if the Fiero had a production run of like 50 years.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B>Yes, but look at the number of members they have... Over 13,000! Wow, I'm impressed!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||HugGRRRFiero||09-19-2000||04:18 PM||||Name: HugGRRRFiero<BR>Date: 9-19-00<BR>Class:  Fiero101<P><B>Problem Statement</B><BR>Determine the number of Pennock's members if the Fiero was in production for 47 years (same as the Corvette 53-00).<P><B>Given Data</B><BR>1,402 current Fiero members - as of this post<BR>13,377 current Corvette members - as of this post<BR>4 years of production - for Fieros<BR>47 years of production - for Corvettes<P><B>Work & Analysis</B><BR>1402/4 = 350.5 members per year produced<P>If we assume the Fiero was produced for 47, same as the Corvette then:<P>47*350.5 = 16,473.5 Fiero members &lt;--<P>16,474-13,377 = 3,097 more Fiero members!<P><B>Results & Conclusion</B><BR>After rounding we would have 16,474 Proud Pennock's Fiero Members!  This means if the Fiero was still in production and stayed in production for the same number of years as the Corvette, then this would be how many members we'd have in the year 2031.  We also find that there would be 3,097 more Fiero members than Corvette members!<P><B>What this all means?</B><BR>GM & Pontiac should have did the math back in 1988.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">       <BR>       <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||whamel||09-19-2000||10:44 PM||||Actually, it means I'll have something else to rub in the face of the guys I used to be in the club with back when I had my Corvette.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks!<BR>Wade<BR>P.S. Yeah!  We win again!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by HugGRRRFiero:<BR><B>Name: HugGRRRFiero<BR>After rounding we would have 16,474 Proud Pennock's Fiero Members!  <BR>[b]What this all means?</B><BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gold-86SE||09-20-2000||09:55 AM||||<BR>There is just one slight problem I noticed with the calculations, which might make alot of difference.<P>Under <B>Given Data</B> the years of production for the Fiero should be 5 not the 4 that is stated.<P>Why? When subtracting 84 from 88 the result is four, but when enumerating the years it is 84, 85, 86, 87, 88 - which totals five.<P>But, even with this difference, I am curious what the numbers look like.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000008||HugGRRRFiero||09-20-2000||10:41 AM||||Oh shucks, good catch Gold-86SE.  I was taking 88-84=4 years.  Awe man, I need to count using my fingers from now on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Using 5 years of Fiero production I now get 13,178.8 or rounding to <B>13,179 Fiero members</B><P>Putting us behind the Corvette members by 198  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  But, looking at the new numbers, there would only be about 1.5% less Fiero members than the Corvette members overall.  That's still pretty close.  I would give at least a +/-5% or +/-10% error in these calculations due to differences in our geo-synchronous orbit around the sun, global warming, stock market, fluctuations in the price of hot dog buns, OPEC, and other environmental factors.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Does this mean I get an <B>F</B> now?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000009||Cliff Pennock||09-20-2000||11:48 AM||||Don't forget Skinner's Constant.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Robert||09-20-2000||07:28 PM||||You also may want to consider the relationship of the number of possible offspring of Corvette owners since 1955 with the number of possible offspring to Fiero owners since 1984.<BR>The way I see, a dude in lets say 1967 with a Corvette had a MUCH better chance at attracting young ladies, than I did with my Nash Rambler.  Therefore, the Corvette driver’s chances of starting a family were much greater to mine.  And when that Rug Rat grew up and wanted a car, what do you suppose would be the car choice, why just like old dads Corvette, of course.  On the other hand, Fiero with its later start in life, i.e. 1984, did not compete with the same set of rules.  The Fiero is of course a babe/dude magnet, but society had changed in the 90’s.   The average family in 1970 had 3.2 children, whereas the average family in 1995 had 1.8 children. This procreation of Corvette kids in the 70’s must have an impact on the total number of members in the Corvette Forum.<P>Just some ramblings  of an old man who owns a Fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||12
